Article 1 Cases directly accepted by a People's Court as private prosecutions include:
(1) Cases to be handled only upon complaint:
1. Cases of insult or defamation (as provided for in Article 246 of the Criminal Law, except those that seriously endanger social order and national interests);
2. Cases of interference with the freedom of marriage by violence (as provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 257 of the Criminal Law);
3. Cases of maltreatment (as provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 260 of the Criminal Law);
4. Cases of embezzlement (as provided for in Article 270 of the Criminal Law).
(2) Minor criminal cases for which the People's Procuratorate has not initiated a public prosecution and the victim has evidence to prove:
1. Cases of intentional injury (as provided for in paragraph 1 of Article 234 of the Criminal Law);
2. Cases of illegal entry into a residence (as provided for in Article 245 of the Criminal Law);
3. Cases of infringement upon the right to freedom of communication (as provided for in Article 252 of the Criminal Law);
4. Cases of bigamy (as provided for in Article 258 of the Criminal Law);
5. Cases of abandonment (as provided for in Article 261 of the Criminal Law);
6. Cases of producing and selling fake or substandard commodities (as provided for in Section 1 of Chapter III of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, except those that seriously endanger social order and national interests);
7. Cases of infringement upon intellectual property rights (as provided for in Section 7 of Chapter III of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, except those that seriously endanger social order and national interests);
8. Cases provided for in Chapter IV and Chapter V of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law for which the defendant may be sentenced to a punishment of not more than three years of fixed-term imprisonment.
For cases specified in this item, if the victim directly files a prosecution with the People's Court, the People's Court shall accept it according to law. For those with insufficient evidence that may be accepted by a public security organ, or for which it is believed that the defendant may be sentenced to a punishment of not less than three years of fixed-term imprisonment, the victim shall be informed to report the case to the public security organ, or the case shall be transferred to the public security organ for filing and investigation.
(3) Cases where the victim has evidence to prove that the defendant's act of infringing upon the victim's personal or property rights shall be subject to criminal liability according to law, and has evidence to prove that a complaint has been filed, but the public security organ or the People's Procuratorate has not investigated the defendant's criminal liability.

Article 15 A Primary People's Court shall transfer to an Intermediate People's Court for trial a criminal case of first instance that may be punishable by life imprisonment or the death penalty.
A Primary People's Court may request to transfer to an Intermediate People's Court for trial any of the following criminal cases of first instance:
(1) Major or complex cases;
(2) New types of difficult cases;
(3) Cases of general guiding significance in the application of law.
If a case needs to be transferred to an Intermediate People's Court for trial, a written request for transfer shall be submitted to the Intermediate People's Court at the latest fifteen days before the expiration of the trial period, after reporting to and obtaining a decision from the president of the court. The Intermediate People's Court shall make a decision within ten days after receiving the request. If the transfer is not approved, a decision disapproving the transfer shall be issued, and the requesting court shall try the case according to law; if the transfer is approved, a decision approving the transfer shall be issued, and the People's Procuratorate at the same level shall be notified in writing.

Article 23 Adjudicatory personnel shall withdraw on their own initiative under any of the following circumstances, and a party or his statutory agent shall have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) They are a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) They or their close relatives have an interest in the case;
(3) They have served as a witness, expert evaluator, defender, litigation agent, or interpreter in the case;
(4) They have a close relative relationship with the defender or litigation agent of the case;
(5) They have other interests with a party to the case that may affect the impartiality of the trial.

Article 24 Where adjudicatory personnel violate regulations by engaging in any of the following acts, a party or his statutory agent shall have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) Meeting with a party, defender, or litigation agent of the case in violation of regulations;
(2) Recommending or introducing a defender or litigation agent for a party to the case, or introducing the handling of the case to a lawyer or other person;
(3) Soliciting or accepting property or other benefits from a party to the case or his client;
(4) Accepting a banquet hosted by a party to the case or his client, or participating in activities paid for by them;
(5) Borrowing money or goods from a party to the case or his client;
(6) Engaging in other improper acts that may affect the impartiality of the trial.

Article 35 When adjudicating a case, a People's Court shall fully guarantee the right to defense enjoyed by the defendant according to law.
In addition to exercising the right of defense on his own, a defendant may also entrust a defender to defend him. The following persons shall not serve as defenders:
(1) Persons who are currently serving a criminal punishment or are in the probation or parole supervision period;
(2) Persons who have been deprived of or restricted of personal freedom according to law;
(3) Persons who have no capacity or have limited capacity for civil conduct;
(4) Active personnel of a People's Court, People's Procuratorate, public security organ, state security organ, or prison;
(5) People's assessors;
(6) Persons who have an interest in the outcome of the trial of the case; and
(7) Foreigners or stateless persons.
If a person specified in items (4) through (7) of the preceding paragraph is the guardian or a close relative of the defendant and is entrusted by the defendant to serve as a defender, such appointment may be permitted.

Article 42 For any of the following defendants who have not entrusted a defender, the People's Court shall notify a legal aid agency to assign a lawyer to provide them with defense:
(1) A blind, deaf, or mute person;
(2) A mental patient who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his own conduct;
(3) A person who may be sentenced to life imprisonment or the death penalty.
When a Higher People's Court reviews a death sentence case, if the defendant has not entrusted a defender, it shall notify a legal aid agency to assign a lawyer to provide him with defense.

Article 43 Under any of the following circumstances where a defendant has not entrusted a defender, the People's Court may notify a legal aid agency to assign a lawyer to provide him with defense:
(1) In a joint crime case, other defendants have already entrusted defenders;
(2) The case has significant social impact;
(3) The case is one in which the People's Procuratorate has filed a protest;
(4) The defendant's conduct may not constitute a crime; or
(5) Other circumstances where it is necessary to assign a lawyer to provide defense.

Article 64 The facts of a case to be proved by evidence include:
(1) The identity of the defendant and the victim;
(2) Whether the alleged crime exists;
(3) Whether the alleged crime was committed by the defendant;
(4) Whether the defendant has criminal capacity, whether there is culpability, and the motive and purpose of committing the crime;
(5) The time, place, means, consequences of the crime, and the cause of the case, etc.;
(6) The status and role of the defendant in a joint crime;
(7) Whether the defendant has circumstances for heavier, lighter, mitigated, or exempted punishment;
(8) Facts concerning collateral civil actions and the disposition of property involved in the case;
(9) Procedural facts concerning jurisdiction, withdrawal, and adjournment of trial, etc.;
(10) Other facts related to conviction and sentencing.
To find the defendant guilty and impose a heavier punishment on him, the standard of proof that the evidence is reliable and sufficient shall apply.

Article 67 The following persons shall not serve as witnesses to criminal proceedings:
(1) Persons who are physically or mentally defective or are young and thus lack the corresponding ability to discern or to express correctly;
(2) Persons who have an interest in the case and may affect the fair handling of the case;
(3) Personnel of public security and judicial organs or persons employed by them who exercise criminal procedural powers such as inquest, examination, search, and seizure.
Where it is impossible due to objective reasons to have a qualified person serve as a witness, the circumstances shall be noted in the written record, and the relevant activities shall be recorded by video.

Article 69 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on physical evidence and documentary evidence:
(1) Whether the physical evidence or documentary evidence is the original object or original document, whether it has been identified or appraised; whether the photographs, videos, copies of physical evidence, or duplicates, copies of documentary evidence are consistent with the original object or original document, whether they were made by two or more persons, and whether there are textual explanations and signatures of the maker regarding the production process and the location where the original object or document is stored;
(2) Whether the collection procedures and methods of physical evidence and documentary evidence comply with laws and relevant provisions; whether the physical evidence and documentary evidence obtained or seized through inquest, examination, search, or seizure are accompanied by relevant records and lists, whether the records and lists are signed by the investigators, the holder of the item, and the witness, and if the holder of the item has not signed, whether the reason is noted; whether the name, characteristics, quantity, quality, etc., of the item are clearly stated;
(3) Whether the physical evidence or documentary evidence has been damaged or altered during the process of collection, preservation, or appraisal;
(4) Whether the physical evidence or documentary evidence is related to the facts of the case; whether biological samples, traces, and items such as blood stains, body fluids, hair, and fingerprints left at the scene related to the crime and suitable for appraisal have been subjected to DNA identification, fingerprint identification, etc., and compared with the corresponding biological samples, biological characteristics, items, etc., of the defendant or the victim;
(5) Whether all physical evidence and documentary evidence related to the facts of the case have been comprehensively collected.

Article 73 Physical evidence or documentary evidence obtained or seized during an inquest, examination, or search, if not accompanied by records or lists and thus unable to prove the source of the physical or documentary evidence, shall not be used as the basis for adjudication.
Where the collection procedures or methods of physical or documentary evidence have the following defects, they may be adopted after correction or a reasonable explanation is provided:
(1) The records of inquest, examination, search, or seizure or the seizure list are not signed by investigators, the holder of the item, or the witness, or the name, characteristics, quantity, quality, etc., of the item are not clearly stated;
(2) The photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence, or the duplicates or copies of documentary evidence do not indicate that they have been verified as consistent with the original, do not have the time of reproduction, or are not signed or sealed by the person who collected or obtained them;
(3) The photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence, or the duplicates or copies of documentary evidence lack an explanation by the maker regarding the production process and the location of the original object or document, or the explanation is not signed;
(4) There are other defects.
If there are doubts about the source or collection procedures of physical or documentary evidence that cannot be reasonably explained, such physical or documentary evidence shall not be used as the basis for adjudication.

Article 74 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on witness testimony:
(1) Whether the content of the testimony was directly perceived by the witness;
(2) Whether the age, cognitive, memory, and expressive abilities, and physical and mental state of the witness at the time of testifying affect the testimony;
(3) Whether the witness has an interest in the parties or the outcome of the case;
(4) Whether the witness was questioned individually;
(5) Whether the making and modification of the interrogation record comply with laws and relevant provisions, whether the start and end times and location of the interrogation are noted, whether the witness was informed of the rights, obligations, and legal liabilities related to testifying at the first interrogation, and whether the witness has checked and confirmed the interrogation record;
(6) When questioning a minor witness, whether the witness's statutory agent or relevant personnel were notified to be present, and whether such persons were actually present;
(7) Whether the witness testimony was collected through illegal methods such as violence or threats;
(8) Whether the testimonies are mutually corroborated and consistent with other evidence, and whether there are contradictions.

Article 76 Witness testimony shall not be used as the basis for adjudication under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The witness was not questioned individually;
(2) The written testimony was not checked and confirmed by the witness;
(3) When questioning a deaf or mute person, an interpreter proficient in sign language should have been provided but was not;
(4) When questioning a witness who does not understand the local commonly used language or script, an interpreter should have been provided but was not.

Article 77 Where the procedures or methods for collecting witness testimony have the following defects, the testimony may be adopted after correction or a reasonable explanation is provided; if no correction or reasonable explanation can be made, it shall not be used as the basis for adjudication:
(1) The interrogation record does not fill in the names of the interrogator, recorder, statutory agent, or the start and end times and location of the interrogation;
(2) The interrogation location does not comply with the provisions;
(3) The interrogation record does not record that the witness was informed of the rights, obligations, and legal liabilities related to testifying;
(4) The interrogation record reflects that during the same time period, the same interrogator questioned different witnesses.

Article 80 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on the defendant's confessions and excuses:
(1) Whether the time, place, identity and number of interrogators, and methods of interrogation comply with laws and relevant provisions;
(2) Whether the making and modification of the interrogation record comply with laws and relevant provisions, whether the specific start and end times and location of the interrogation are noted, whether the defendant was informed of the relevant rights and legal provisions at the first interrogation, and whether the defendant checked and confirmed the record;
(3) When interrogating a minor defendant, whether the statutory agent or relevant personnel were notified to be present, and whether such persons were actually present;
(4) Whether the defendant's confession was obtained through illegal methods such as extorting confessions by torture;
(5) Whether the defendant's confessions are consistent, whether there are inconsistencies and the reasons for such inconsistencies; whether all of the defendant's confessions and excuses have been transferred with the case file;
(6) Whether the content of the defendant's excuses is consistent with the circumstances of the case and common sense, and whether there are contradictions;
(7) Whether the defendant's confessions and excuses are mutually corroborated and consistent with those of co-defendants and other evidence, and whether there are contradictions.
If necessary, the audio and video recordings of the interrogation process, the health examination records of the defendant when entering and leaving the detention center, and the records may be obtained and used, in conjunction with the recordings, records, and notes, to examine the above content.

Article 81 A defendant's confession shall not be used as the basis for adjudication under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The interrogation record was not checked and confirmed by the defendant;
(2) When interrogating a deaf or mute person, an interpreter proficient in sign language should have been provided but was not;
(3) When interrogating a defendant who does not understand the local commonly used language or script, an interpreter should have been provided but was not.

Article 82 Where the interrogation record has the following defects, it may be adopted after correction or a reasonable explanation is provided; if no correction or reasonable explanation can be made, it shall not be used as the basis for adjudication:
(1) The interrogation time, interrogator, recorder, statutory agent, etc., filled in the interrogation record are incorrect or contradictory;
(2) The interrogator did not sign;
(3) The first interrogation record does not record that the person being interrogated was informed of the relevant rights and legal provisions.

Article 84 The following content shall be examined with emphasis on expert opinions:
(1) Whether the expert institution and the expert have the statutory qualifications;
(2) Whether the expert has circumstances requiring withdrawal;
(3) Whether the source, acquisition, preservation, and submission of the sample for examination comply with laws and relevant provisions, whether they are consistent with the content recorded in the relevant extraction records, seizure lists, etc., and whether the sample is sufficient and reliable;
(4) Whether the formal requirements of the expert opinion are complete, whether it states the reasons for initiating the examination, the client, the expert institution, the examination requirements, the examination process, the examination method, the examination date, etc., and whether it is affixed with the judicial examination special seal by the expert institution and signed and sealed by the expert;
(5) Whether the examination procedure complies with laws and relevant provisions;
(6) Whether the examination process and method comply with the specifications of the relevant profession;
(7) Whether the expert opinion is clear;
(8) Whether the expert opinion is related to the facts to be proved in the case;
(9) Whether the expert opinion contradicts other evidence such as inquest and examination records and related photographs;
(10) Whether the expert opinion was promptly informed to the relevant persons according to law, and whether the parties have any objection to the expert opinion.

Article 85 An expert opinion shall not be used as the basis for adjudication under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The expert institution does not have the statutory qualifications, or the subject of the examination exceeds the business scope or technical conditions of the expert institution;
(2) The expert does not have the statutory qualifications, does not have the relevant professional expertise or title, or has violated the provisions on withdrawal;
(3) The source of the submitted material or sample is unknown, or it does not meet the conditions for examination due to contamination;
(4) The object of examination is inconsistent with the submitted material or sample;
(5) The examination procedure violates the provisions;
(6) The examination process and method do not comply with the specifications of the relevant profession;
(7) The expert opinion document lacks a signature or seal;
(8) The expert opinion is not related to the facts to be proved in the case;
(9) Other circumstances that violate relevant provisions.

Article 88. The following contents shall be emphatically examined in the record of inquest and inspection:
(1) Whether the inquest and inspection are conducted according to law, whether the preparation of the record complies with the law and relevant provisions, and whether the inquest and inspection personnel and witnesses have signed or affixed seals;
(2) Whether the record of inquest and inspection records the cause of initiating the inquest and inspection, the time and place of the inquest and inspection, the persons present, scene orientation, surrounding environment, etc., the positions and characteristics of objects, persons, corpses, etc. at the scene, and the process of inquest, inspection, and search; whether the written record is consistent with the physical objects, drawings, photographs, or video recordings; whether the scene, objects, traces, etc. are forged or damaged; whether physical characteristics, injuries, and physiological conditions are disguised or changed, etc.;
(3) If a supplementary inquest or inspection is conducted, whether the reason for the re-inquest or inspection is explained, and whether the circumstances of the previous and subsequent inquests or inspections are contradictory.

Article 90. The process and method of identification and whether the preparation of the identification record complies with relevant provisions shall be emphatically examined.
An identification record shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The identification is not conducted under the auspices of investigation personnel;
(2) The identifier is allowed to see the identification object before the identification;
(3) The identification activities are not conducted separately;
(4) The identification object is not mixed with other objects having similar characteristics, or the number of objects presented for identification does not comply with the provisions;
(5) Obvious hints are given to the identifier during the identification, or there is obvious suspicion of directed identification;
(6) Other circumstances where relevant provisions are violated and the authenticity of the identification record cannot be determined.

Article 92. The following contents shall be emphatically examined in audio-visual materials:
(1) Whether a description of the extraction process is attached, and whether the source is lawful;
(2) Whether they are originals, and whether there are copies and the number of copies; if they are copies, whether an explanation of the reason for the inability to obtain the originals, the production process of the copies, and the location of the originals is attached, and whether the producer and the original holder of the audio-visual materials have signed or affixed seals;
(3) Whether there are circumstances such as threats or inducement of parties during the production process that violate the law or relevant provisions;
(4) Whether the identities of the producer and holder, and the time, place, conditions, and method of production are indicated;
(5) Whether the content and production process are authentic, and whether there are circumstances such as editing, addition, deletion, or alteration;
(6) Whether the content is related to the facts of the case.
If there is doubt about the audio-visual materials, an expert evaluation shall be conducted.

Article 93. The following contents shall be emphatically examined in electronic data such as emails, electronic data interchange, online chat records, blogs, microblogs, mobile phone messages, electronic signatures, and domain names:
(1) Whether they are transferred together with the original storage medium; when the original storage medium cannot be sealed, is inconvenient to move, or shall be kept, handled, or returned by relevant departments according to law, whether the extraction or copying of electronic data is conducted by two or more persons, whether it is sufficient to ensure the integrity of the electronic data, and whether there is a written description and signatures of the extraction or copying process and the location of the original storage medium;
(2) Whether the collection procedures and methods comply with the law and relevant technical specifications; for electronic data collected through inquest, inspection, search and other investigation activities, whether records and lists are attached and signed by investigation personnel, the electronic data holder, and witnesses; if there is no signature of the holder, whether the reason is indicated; for remotely obtaining electronic data from abroad or another location, whether the relevant circumstances are indicated; whether the specifications, categories, file formats, etc. of the electronic data are clearly indicated;
(3) Whether the content of the electronic data is authentic, and whether there are circumstances such as deletion, modification, or addition;
(4) Whether the electronic data is related to the facts of the case;
(5) Whether the electronic data related to the facts of the case is comprehensively collected.
If there is doubt about the electronic data, an expert evaluation or examination shall be conducted.

Article 94. Audio-visual materials or electronic data shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) Their authenticity cannot be determined after examination;
(2) There is doubt about the time, place, method, etc. of their production or acquisition, and the necessary proof cannot be provided or a reasonable explanation cannot be made.

Article 103. Under any of the following circumstances, the second instance People's Court shall examine the legality of evidence collection and handle the matter in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Law and the relevant provisions of this Interpretation:
(1) The first instance People's Court did not examine the application of the party, his or her defender, or litigation agent to exclude illegally obtained evidence, and used such evidence as the basis for deciding the case;
(2) The People's Procuratorate or the defendant, the private prosecutor, or his or her statutory agent, dissatisfied with the investigation conclusion made by the first instance People's Court concerning the legality of evidence collection, files a protest or appeal;
(3) The party, his or her defender, or litigation agent discovers relevant clues or materials only after the conclusion of the first instance and applies to the People's Court to exclude illegally obtained evidence.

Article 105. Where there is no direct evidence, but the circumstantial evidence concurrently meets the following conditions, the defendant may be found guilty:
(1) The evidence has been verified as true;
(2) The evidence corroborates each other, and there are no irresolvable contradictions or inexplicable doubts;
(3) All evidence in the case has formed a complete proof system;
(4) Determining the facts of the case based on the evidence is sufficient to exclude reasonable doubt, and the conclusion is unique;
(5) The reasoning using the evidence conforms to logic and experience.

Article 109. The following evidence shall be used with caution, and may be accepted if corroborated by other evidence:
(1) Statements, testimonies, and confessions made by victims, witnesses, or defendants who have physical or mental defects, have some difficulty in perceiving or expressing the facts of the case, but have not yet lost the ability to correctly perceive and express;
(2) Testimonies favorable to the defendant made by witnesses who have a familial relationship or other close relationship with the defendant, or testimonies unfavorable to the defendant made by witnesses who have a conflict of interest with the defendant.

Article 117. Where release on bail pending investigation or trial is decided for a defendant falling under any of the following categories, the defendant may be ordered to provide one or two guarantors:
(1) The defendant is unable to pay the deposit;
(2) The defendant is a minor or has reached the age of seventy-five;
(3) Other defendants for whom it is inappropriate to collect a deposit.

Article 129 Where a defendant released on bail pending investigation or trial falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall decide to arrest:
(1) intentionally committing a new crime;
(2) attempting suicide or escape;
(3) destroying or forging evidence, interfering with witness testimony or colluding to make false statements;
(4) retaliating against the victim, informant, or accuser;
(5) failing to appear in court without justifiable reasons after being summoned, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings;
(6) arbitrarily changing contact information or place of residence, resulting in inability to summon, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings;
(7) leaving the city or county where he or she resides without approval, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has, on two occasions, left the city or county where he or she resides without approval;
(8) entering a specific place, meeting or communicating with specific persons, or engaging in specific activities in violation of regulations, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has on two occasions violated the relevant provisions;
(9) any other circumstance under which an arrest shall be decided according to law.

Article 130 Where a defendant under residential surveillance falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall decide to arrest:
(1) falling under any of the circumstances specified in items (1) through (5) of the preceding Article;
(2) leaving the place where residential surveillance is executed without approval, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has, on two occasions, left the place where residential surveillance is executed without approval;
(3) meeting or communicating with others without approval, thereby affecting the normal conduct of judicial proceedings, or has, on two occasions, met or communicated with others without approval;
(4) in the case of a defendant who was not arrested due to serious illness or inability to take care of himself or herself, or due to pregnancy or being currently nursing an infant, the illness has been cured or the nursing period has expired;
(5) any other circumstance under which an arrest shall be decided according to law.

Article 133 Where an arrested defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may change the compulsory measure:
(1) suffering from a serious illness and being unable to take care of himself or herself;
(2) being pregnant or currently nursing an infant;
(3) being the sole supporter of a person who is unable to take care of himself or herself.

Article 134 Where a court of first instance sentences a defendant to be not guilty, not to bear criminal liability, or to be exempted from criminal punishment, and the defendant is in custody, the defendant shall be released immediately after the pronouncement of judgment.
Where an arrested defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall change the compulsory measure or release the defendant:
(1) the court of first instance imposes public surveillance, pronounces a suspended sentence, or imposes solely a supplementary punishment, and the judgment has not yet become legally effective;
(2) the period during which the defendant has been held in custody has reached the term of the sentence imposed by the court of first instance;
(3) the case cannot be concluded within the time limit prescribed by law.

Article 143 Persons who are liable for compensation in an incidental civil action according to law include:
(1) the criminal defendant and other joint infringers who are not subject to criminal liability;
(2) the guardian of the criminal defendant;
(3) the heir to the estate of a criminal sentenced to death;
(4) the heir to the estate of a defendant in a joint crime case who dies before the conclusion of the case;
(5) other entities or individuals that shall bear compensation liability for the victim's material losses according to law.
Where a friend or relative of the defendant in an incidental civil action voluntarily pays compensation on behalf of the defendant, such compensation shall be permitted.

Article 145 The conditions for bringing an incidental civil action are:
(1) the plaintiff meets the statutory conditions;
(2) there is a clear defendant;
(3) there are specific claims for compensation and the facts and reasons therefor;
(4) the case falls within the scope of incidental civil actions accepted by the People's Court.

Article 180 For a case in which a public prosecution has been initiated, after receiving the indictment (eight copies, plus five copies for each additional defendant) and the case file and evidence, the People's Court shall designate an adjudicator to examine the following:
(1) whether the case is within the jurisdiction of that court;
(2) whether the indictment states the identity of the defendant, whether the defendant has been or is currently subject to criminal punishment, the type of compulsory measures taken, the place of custody, the time, place, means, and consequences of the crime, and other circumstances that may affect conviction and sentencing;
(3) whether evidence materials proving the alleged criminal facts have been transferred, including the approval decision for technical investigation measures and the evidence materials collected;
(4) whether the defendant's illegal gains or other property involved in the case have been sealed, seized, or frozen, and whether evidence materials proving that such property shall be recovered according to law are attached;
(5) whether the name, address, and contact information of the victim are listed; whether a list of witnesses and expert evaluators is attached; whether there is a request for the court to notify witnesses, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court, listing the names, gender, age, occupation, address, and contact information of such persons; whether a list of witnesses, expert evaluators, and victims requiring protection is attached;
(6) where a party has retained a defender or litigation agent, or has accepted legal aid, whether the names, addresses, and contact information of the defender and litigation agent are listed;
(7) whether an incidental civil action has been initiated; if an incidental civil action has been initiated, whether the names, addresses, and contact information of the parties to the incidental civil action are listed and whether relevant evidence materials are attached;
(8) whether the various legal procedures and litigation documents of the investigation and examination for prosecution stages are complete;
(9) whether there are circumstances under which criminal liability shall not be investigated as provided in paragraphs 2 through 6 of Article 15 of the Criminal Procedure Law.

Article 181 After examining a case in which a public prosecution has been initiated, the People's Court shall handle it according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the case is one that shall only be handled upon complaint, the court shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate and inform the victim of the right to initiate a private prosecution;
(2) if the case is not within its jurisdiction or the defendant is not present, the court shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) if the case does not meet any of the requirements under paragraphs 2 through 8 of the preceding article and supplementary materials are needed, the court shall notify the People's Procuratorate to supplement the materials within three days;
(4) if, after a defendant is acquitted in accordance with paragraph 3 of Article 195 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the People's Procuratorate re-institutes prosecution based on new facts or evidence, the court shall accept the case according to law;
(5) if a case has been permitted to withdraw the prosecution by ruling in accordance with Article 242 of this Interpretation, and the People's Procuratorate re-institutes prosecution without new facts or evidence, the court shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(6) if the circumstances under paragraphs 2 through 6 of Article 15 of the Criminal Procedure Law are present, the court shall rule to terminate the adjudication or return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(7) if the true identity of the defendant is unknown but the conditions under paragraph 2 of Article 158 of the Criminal Procedure Law are met, the court shall accept the case according to law.
The examination of whether to accept a public prosecution case shall be completed within seven days.

Article 182 Before the trial session begins, the People's Court shall carry out the following:
(1) determine the presiding judge and the composition of the collegial panel;
(2) serve a copy of the indictment on the defendant and defender ten days before the trial session;
(3) notify the parties, legal representatives, defenders, and litigation agents to provide, five days before the trial session, the list of witnesses and expert evaluators as well as the evidence intended to be presented in court; if applying for witnesses, expert evaluators, or persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court, the names, gender, age, occupation, address, and contact information of such persons shall be listed;
(4) notify the People's Procuratorate of the time and place of the trial session three days before the trial session;
(5) serve the summons to the parties and the notices to the defenders, litigation agents, legal representatives, witnesses, expert evaluators, and others to appear in court three days before the trial session; such notices may also be sent by telephone, text message, fax, email, or other means that can confirm receipt;
(6) in cases to be tried in public, announce the cause of action, defendant's name, trial time, and place three days before the trial session.
The above work shall be recorded in the case file.

Article 183 If a case has any of the following circumstances, the adjudicators may convene a pre-trial conference:
(1) a party, its defender, or litigation agent applies to exclude illegally obtained evidence;
(2) there is a large amount of evidence materials and the case is major and complex;
(3) the case has significant social impact;
(4) other circumstances requiring a pre-trial conference.
When convening a pre-trial conference, the defendant may be notified to participate depending on the circumstances of the case.

Article 184 When convening a pre-trial conference, the adjudicators may inquire of the prosecution and the defense about the following issues and hear their opinions:
(1) whether there is any objection to the jurisdiction of the case;
(2) whether an application is made for the withdrawal of relevant personnel;
(3) whether an application is made to obtain evidence materials collected by the public security organ or the People's Procuratorate during investigation or examination for prosecution but not transferred with the case file that prove the defendant's innocence or lesser guilt;
(4) whether new evidence will be provided;
(5) whether there is any objection to the list of witnesses, expert evaluators, or persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court;
(6) whether an application is made to exclude illegally obtained evidence;
(7) whether an application is made for closed trial;
(8) other issues related to the trial.
The adjudicators may ask the prosecution and defense whether they have any objections to the evidence materials; for evidence objected to, the court shall focus its investigation during the trial; for evidence not objected to, the presentation and examination of evidence during trial may be simplified.
If the victim or his or her legal representative or near relative files an incidental civil action, mediation may be conducted.
A record shall be made of the pre-trial conference.

Article 185 Before the trial session begins, the collegial panel may prepare a trial outline, which generally includes the following:
(1) the division of duties among the members of the collegial panel during trial;
(2) the key points of the criminal facts alleged in the indictment and the main points for determining the nature of the case;
(3) the main points of the case facts that need to be understood when interrogating the defendant;
(4) the list of witnesses, expert evaluators, persons with specialized knowledge, and investigators appearing in court;
(5) the catalog of evidence that the prosecution and defense apply to present in court;
(6) potential issues that may arise during trial and countermeasures.

Article 189 Before the trial session begins, the court clerk shall carry out the following in order:
(1) upon the entrustment of the presiding judge, ascertain whether the public prosecutor, parties, witnesses, and other litigation participants are present;
(2) read the court rules;
(3) invite the public prosecutor and relevant litigation participants to enter the courtroom;
(4) invite the presiding judge, judges (people's assessors) to enter the courtroom;
(5) after the adjudicators are seated, report to the presiding judge that preparations before trial are complete.

Article 190 After the presiding judge announces the opening of the trial session and summons the defendant into court, he or she shall ascertain the following about the defendant:
(1) the defendant's name, date of birth, ethnicity, place of birth, education level, occupation, address, or the name and domicile of the defendant entity, and the name and position of its litigation representative;
(2) whether the defendant has received any legal sanction and the type and time of such sanction;
(3) whether compulsory measures have been taken and the type and time of such measures;
(4) the date of receipt of the copy of the indictment; if there is an incidental civil action, the date on which the defendant in the incidental civil action received the incidental civil complaint.
If there are many defendants, the above may be ascertained before the trial session, but the presiding judge shall explain this at the opening of the trial.

Article 193 The presiding judge shall inform the parties, their legal representatives, defenders, and litigation agents that during the court proceedings they are entitled according to law to the following procedural rights:
(1) they may apply for the withdrawal of a member of the collegial panel, the court clerk, the public prosecutor, an expert evaluator, or an interpreter;
(2) they may present evidence, apply for notification of new witnesses to appear in court, obtain new evidence, or apply for a new expert evaluation or inspection/examination;
(3) the defendant may conduct his or her own defense;
(4) the defendant may make a final statement after the conclusion of the court debate.

Article 206 If a witness is unable to testify in court due to any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may permit him or her not to appear in court:
(1) suffering from a serious illness or having great difficulty with mobility during the trial period;
(2) residing far from the trial location and having extremely inconvenient transportation;
(3) being abroad and unable to return within a short period;
(4) having other objective reasons that genuinely prevent him or her from appearing in court.
In the circumstances specified in the preceding paragraph, the witness may testify by video or other means.

Article 213 Questioning of a witness shall follow these rules:
(1) the content of the question shall be related to the facts of the case;
(2) leading questions shall not be used;
(3) the witness shall not be threatened;
(4) the dignity of the witness shall not be impaired.
The provisions of the preceding paragraph apply to the interrogation or questioning of the defendant, victim, parties to an incidental civil action, expert evaluators, and persons with specialized knowledge.

Article 225 During trial, facts and evidence related to sentencing shall be investigated.
In addition to examining whether the defendant has statutory sentencing circumstances, the People's Court shall also examine the following circumstances affecting sentencing based on the case:
(1) The cause of the case;
(2) Whether the victim is at fault and the degree of fault, and whether the victim bears responsibility for the escalation of the conflict and the extent of such responsibility;
(3) Whether the defendant's close relatives assisted in apprehending the defendant;
(4) The defendant's usual conduct and whether the defendant shows remorse;
(5) The return of stolen goods, restitution, and compensation;
(6) Whether the defendant has obtained forgiveness from the victim or the victim's close relatives;
(7) Other circumstances affecting sentencing.

Article 229 Court debate shall be conducted under the direction of the presiding judge in the following order:
(1) Statement by the public prosecutor;
(2) Statement by the victim and the victim's litigation agent;
(3) Self-defense by the defendant;
(4) Defense by the defender;
(5) Debate between the prosecution and the defense.

Article 241 For first-instance public prosecution cases, after trial, the People's Court shall render a judgment or ruling according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts charged in the indictment are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the crime charged against the defendant is established pursuant to law, a guilty verdict shall be rendered;
(2) If the facts charged in the indictment are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, but the charged crime is inconsistent with the crime determined through trial, a guilty verdict shall be rendered based on the crime determined through trial;
(3) If the facts of the case are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the defendant is found not guilty pursuant to law, a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant;
(4) If the evidence is insufficient and the defendant cannot be found guilty, a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant on the grounds of insufficient evidence and that the charged crime cannot be established;
(5) If part of the facts of the case are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient, a guilty or not guilty verdict shall be rendered for that part; for the part where the facts are unclear or evidence is insufficient, no finding shall be made;
(6) If the defendant is under the age of 16 and is not subject to criminal punishment, a judgment shall be rendered declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability;
(7) If the defendant is a mental patient who caused harmful consequences when unable to recognize or control his or her own conduct, and is not subject to criminal punishment, a judgment shall be rendered declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability;
(8) If the statute of limitations for prosecution has expired and prosecution is not mandatory, or if the punishment has been remitted by a special amnesty order, a ruling shall be rendered to terminate the trial;
(9) If the defendant has died, a ruling shall be rendered to terminate the trial; however, if based on the facts of the case that have been ascertained and the evidence that has been determined, the defendant can be confirmed to be not guilty, a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant.
Where the circumstances provided for in subparagraph (2) of the preceding paragraph exist, the People's Court shall, before rendering the judgment, hear the opinions of both the prosecution and the defense, and ensure that the defendant and defender fully exercise the right of defense. If necessary, the court may reopen the trial and organize the prosecution and defense to debate over what crime the defendant's conduct constitutes.

Article 249 During trial, participants in the proceedings and spectators shall observe the following discipline:
(1) Comply with the direction of the court and observe courtroom etiquette;
(2) Refrain from applauding, making noise, causing disturbance, or walking around arbitrarily;
(3) Refrain from making audio recordings, video recordings, or photographs of the trial activities, or disseminating the trial situation by sending emails, blogs, microblogs, or other means, except for journalists permitted by the People's Court;
(4) Spectators shall not speak or ask questions;
(5) Refrain from committing any other acts that disrupt courtroom order.

Article 250 During trial, if a participant in the proceedings or a spectator disrupts courtroom order, the presiding judge shall handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the circumstances are relatively minor, the presiding judge shall give a warning to stop the conduct and administer a reprimand;
(2) If the person refuses to stop after being warned, the presiding judge may instruct a judicial police officer to forcibly remove the person from the courtroom;
(3) If the circumstances are serious, upon approval by the president of the court, the person may be fined not more than 1,000 yuan or detained for not more than 15 days;
(4) If the person makes audio recordings, video recordings, or photographs without permission, or disseminates the trial situation by sending emails, blogs, microblogs, or other means, the storage medium or relevant equipment may be temporarily seized.
If a participant in the proceedings or spectator disagrees with the decision of a fine or detention, the person may directly apply for reconsideration to the People's Court at the next higher level, or may apply for reconsideration through the People's Court that made the decision on the fine or detention. If the person applies for reconsideration through the People's Court that made the decision, that People's Court shall, within three days from receipt of the reconsideration application, submit the reconsideration application, the decision on fine or detention, and the relevant facts and evidentiary materials to the People's Court at the next higher level for reconsideration. During the period of reconsideration, the execution of the decision shall not be suspended.
